mplex  systems can have unpredictably large  effects the world of nations is perhaps  not quite as intricate as natural as the  natural world but it certainly holds the  potential for great surprise merely  itemizing the foreseeable effects of war  with Iraq suggest reverberations that  would be felt for decades if we can  judge from past Wars the effects we  can't imagine when the fighting begins  will prove to be the ones that matter  most thank you  you have three minutes to read the whole  thing you know both mark and Jim  referred to getting phone calls from  Mike Kelly the day after 9/11 and the  day of 9/11 and and I think William  langa V was also on the receiving end of  a phone call from Mike who was the  editor in chief and Cullen Murphy the  managing editor at that time and the  purpose of the call of course was to  hash out exactly how he might best  contribute to the magazine's coverage of  the tragedy and it's aftermath one  option was to fly William who was at  home in Northern California to Pakistan  from which he would make his way into  Afghanistan and join up with the  Northern Alliance as it waged war  against Al Qaeda's Taliban sponsors and  another idea was to dispatch him to New  York City to chronicle the frenetic  rescue and recovery operation that was  then already well underway amid the  ruins of the World Trade Center as soon  as the ban on air travel was lifted  William flew to New York and took a  quick reading of the disaster scene  trying to determine which municipal  efficients officials and agencies would  be entrusted with the huge job of  removing the tons of rubble and locating  the thousands of victims lying buried  deep within  at first William was stymied in his  efforts to secure access to the site  both by stonewalling officials of the  city's office of emergency management  the agency ostensibly in charge of the  operation of the rescue  recovery and by the legions of reporters  who were queuing up for press  credentials at police headquarters but  there was a piece of serendipity here  because it turned out that a man by the  name of Kenneth Holden who was the  Commissioner of a very of a relatively  small little known city agency called  the Department of design and  construction was a longtime fan of  Williams and this particular agency was  emerging as the de facto control as the  de facto supervisor of the ground zero  operations for the next nine months  William Langevin was the sole journalist  to have not only round-the-clock freedom  to interview supervisors contractors and  workers excavating the so-called pile  but also unrestricted entree to prolong  strategy sessions and seat-of-the-pants  problem-solving meet meetings among the  key players it's interesting that for  all the devastation and carnage that  William encountered day after day on the  pile he found in a surprisingly  inspiring story to tell one of  extraordinary resourcefulness and  resilience and in this excerpt that's in  the anthology which represents the sum  of the first installment of a three-part  article which at 70,000 words was the  longest piece that the Atlanta of  original reporting that the Atlantic had  ever published  William ended his writing this way  despite the epochal quote despite the  apocalyptic nature of the scene the  response was on hesitant and almost  childishly optimistic it was simply  understood that you would find survivors  and then you would find the dead and  that you would work night and day to  clean up the mess and that this would  allow the world's greatest city to  rebuild quickly  and maybe even make itself into  something better than before these are  the last words of the anthology and this  is the last piece in the book and I  think it's completely fitting that this  closed the book and I'm just a great  pleasure to have William Longo viche  here with us tonight thank you thank you  so we can relive our our glories here  yeah this some it was an odd piece but  let 