nal garbage and  you know it was big garbage but it was  garbage it was it was like writing about  a junk pile and well they they wanted me  to do it and when the access occurred  much to our surprise then the typical by  this time it was really Mike Kelly with  all of his energy and ambition for the  magazine and it was just the green light  we don't care how long it takes we don't  care how much it cost we don't care that  we know none of us knew what was going  on what would happen but was what was  actually was going to be interesting no  idea we just knew that we had access and  so we proceeded I'll read a little bit  from it I think as far as the the the  techniques that I used in this case it's  probably natural to me in that I when I  I snuck into the site and basically and  before meeting before sending the fax to  ken hold on I don't know who this guy  was but I figured like I saw all of this  emotion and I I didn't feel very  emotional about what had happened I mean  it was a bad thing and all that but I  wasn't blown out of the water by the  fall of Twin Towers nor by the attacks  of 9/11 personally it was just war and I  saw that once I got past the soldiers  the National Guard who was they would  protect  aside from evil people like me and and I  realized that you know bless the hearts  of all the people who are digging with  shovels and their hands but it wasn't  doing you any good because the debris  was too heavy so if you're gonna find  the living and that was the attempt at  the time people thought there would be  the living trapped inside turned out not  to be true but she had to bring in heavy  equipment and so I asked who's bringing  in the heavy equipment and somebody said  this thing's all the DDC and we went  from there but the the the this sort of  sort of the source of that of that idea  of asking who's bringing the heavy  equipment I described here briefly and I  write here people who came to the site  in those early days often had the same  first sensation of leaving the city and  walking into a dream many also felt when  when they saw the extent of the  destruction that they had stumbled into  a war zone it's like something you'd see  in the movies people said probably so  but my own reaction was different when I  first went in soon after the attacks  after years of traveling through the  back corners of the world I had an  unexpected sense not of the strangeness  of this scene but of its of its  familiarity wading through the debris on  the streets climbing through the newly  torn landscapes breathing in the mixture  of smoke and dust It was as if I had  wandered wandered again into the special  havoc that failing societies tend to  visit upon themselves this time they had  visited it upon us the message seemed to  be here is a sample of our political  science I was impressed by how  faithfully the effects had been  reproduced on the ground now of course  this was not the Middle East this was  this was New York City and New York City  I mean unfortunately for the rest of the  United States is not the United States I  mean the New York City is New York City  it is the supreme the ultimate city on  earth and the what you all know of  course and so what happened there was a  he was very American but it was also  very New York and I don't  believe now looking back on it that the  reactions that I chronicled in this  series would have occurred elsewhere in  the United States we had we have today  huge evidence of the cowardice  that occurred in the rest of the country  but what I saw was not cowardice and  inside the opaque walls beyond all of  the hype about heroism was this  beautiful experiment in solving problems  in solving a very serious problem I'll  read you a little bit about that see in  other countries learn at committees  would have been formed in high  authorities consulted the ruins would  have been pondered in a tightly scripted  response would have been imposed barring  that soldiers w